<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Ja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Ja, also called Jatau, was a ruler of the Hausa dynasty of Zazzau driven into exile by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a>. After his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-makau">Muhammadu Makau</a> fled south to Zuba and died there, Abu Ja succeeded him and around 1828 founded a new walled town that took his name, Abuja. It became the capital of the exiled dynasty's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Ja was born Jatau, a son of Ishaku Jatau, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> state of Zazzau. His mother died soon after his birth, and he was brought up by his father's first wife, Tasalla, as her own child, alongside her sons Makau and Abu Kwaka. The name Abu Ja, Abu the Red, was a nickname given for his light skin.</p><p>In 1804 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> reached Zazzau when Malam Musa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar loyal to Usman, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>. Makau, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> ruler of the state, took refuge in the south-west among his former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, the Koro, and Abu Ja followed him with three thousand people to the Koro town of Zuba, from where they held out against the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Zaria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1825 Makau led an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> town of Lapai, but his soldiers, angry that he had forbidden slave-raiding on an earlier campaign, deserted him in the battle. He named Abu Ja his successor and was killed. Abu Ja spent the first three years of his reign at Izom among the Gbagyi people, then chose a site further from Lapai at the foot of the Abuchi hills, where he built his house in 1828 and began the town's defensive walls a year later. The town soon gained influence over the neighbouring peoples. Among his symbols of office was a quiver of two hundred poisoned arrows made for him by the people of Burum.</p><p>Some years later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Zazzau, Mamman Sani, camped outside the town but left after talks, saying he had come to fight pagans, not the people of Abuja. Abu Ja nonetheless grew suspicious, barred traders and strangers from the town and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> found nearby killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1851 forces of Nassarawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontagora">Kontagora</a>, both owing allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, attacked the people of Toto, and Abu Ja sent men, among them his sons, to their aid. Three of his sons were captured and killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Nassarawa, Makama Dogo, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umaru-nagwamatse">Umaru Nagwamatse</a>, the Emir of Kontagora, had demanded their release. Abu Ja died soon afterwards, on 1 August 1851.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Ja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death his brother <b>Abu Ja</b>, also called Jatau, founded a new walled town around 1828, which took his name, Abuja, and became the capital of the exiled dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/abuja-emirate">Abuja Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
